That breakdown is **mostly mathematically correct**, and it clarifies why your original wording was defensible. I would revise my earlier criticism: calling Liouville’s result a “sharp boundary” is reasonable in an explanatory sense, but it should not imply that Liouville’s inequality completely separates all algebraic numbers from all transcendental numbers.

The key distinction is this. If (\alpha) is an irrational algebraic number of degree (n), Liouville’s theorem gives a constant (c(\alpha)>0) such that

[
\left|\alpha-\frac{p}{q}\right|>\frac{c(\alpha)}{q^n}
]

for rational (p/q) with (q>0). Thus, an algebraic number of fixed degree cannot have rational approximations that repeatedly violate this lower bound.

Liouville then constructed numbers such as

[
L=\sum_{k=1}^{\infty}10^{-k!}
]

whose rational approximations are **far too accurate** to satisfy such a bound for any finite algebraic degree. Therefore (L) must be transcendental.

The important qualification is that **not every transcendental number is a Liouville number**. Transcendental numbers do not, as a class, necessarily admit extraordinarily good rational approximations. In fact, most do not. So the boundary established by Liouville is a powerful **sufficient test for transcendence**, not a complete characterization of transcendental numbers.

